When publishing academic manuscripts with active dual affiliations at a corporate lab ([Microsoft Research](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/)) and an academic institution ([Brandeis University](https://www.brandeis.edu/)), specific policy boundaries must be followed. [1, 2] 
## 1. Author Block Protocol for Double-Blind Review

* Anonymization Stage: For initial peer review submissions to NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, or CVPR/ICCV, the author section must remain fully anonymized to avoid desk rejection. Use the native template tags to strip identifiers completely: \author{Anonymous Author(s)}.
* Camera-Ready Stage: Once accepted, add your distinct dual institutional lines. Order your affiliations based on where the majority of the computational funding or hands-on research occurred. If the project was a joint effort, list both with independent superscript notation markers. [1, 2, 3] 

## 2. Corporate Pre-Publication Intellectual Property Clearance

* MSR Review Pipeline: Prior to submitting your anonymized manuscript to an open server (like arXiv) or a conference portal (OpenReview), you must route the paper through the Microsoft Research Internal Review & Clearance pipeline. This step checks that no proprietary backend configurations, product keys, or customer metrics are accidentally exposed.
* Open Source Policy: Code templates utilizing libraries like DSPy or LangGraph must be cleared for open-source publication under a standard permissive license (such as [MIT](https://opensource.org/license/mit) or [Apache 2.0](https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0)) before submission.

## 3. Open Access, Funding Acknowledgments, & Copyright Formatting

* IEEE/CVF Rules: Conferences sponsored by the [Computer Vision Foundation (CVF)](https://www.thecvf.com/) publish papers under an Open Access model via the official CVF Open Access Repository. [4] 
* Copyright Transfer: Accepted papers undergo an automated copyright management check. If the work was fully or partially executed during your active hours as a corporate employee, the copyright block often reads: Copyright by Microsoft Corporation and Brandeis University.
* Funding Context Footnote: Use an unnumbered footnote on page one via \thanks{} to detail hardware allocations or grant funding transparently, keeping academic and corporate support separate:

\thanks{This work was supported in part by Azure Compute Credits provided by Microsoft Research. Academic validation tools were managed through Brandeis University Data Services.}


