\begin{abstract}
While large language model (LLM) agents are increasingly deployed to execute complex, sequential analytical operations over enterprise data lakehouses, their multi-turn alignment characteristics remain poorly understood. In this work, we formalize the phenomenon of semantic drift---the progressive accumulation of stochastic information decay across independent agent execution boundaries. We introduce the Semantic Trust Index ($\STI$), an information-theoretic metric that tracks latent hidden representation trajectories directly inside model attention horizons. Furthermore, we present a Governance-Aware Semantic Compiler (GASC) to optimize programmatic prompt compiler workloads under strict row and column access control policies. Empirical evaluations using the Spider 2.0 and AgentBench corpuses demonstrate that our dual framework reduces total prompt token consumption by 65\% while mitigating downstream visual chart interpretation hallucinations by up to 42\%.
\end{abstract}

