\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The integration of foundational large language models (LLMs) into autonomous multi-agent orchestration frameworks has enabled a paradigm shift in data engineering. Modern enterprise data lakehouses leverage programmatic prompt compilers, such as DSPy and LangGraph sub-graphs, to automate high-dimensional database interactions, programmatic schema transformations, and complex visual analytics. In these pipelines, localized tasks are decomposed and passed sequentially through a series of specialized nodes. Each autonomous agent consumes the generative outputs of its processor predecessor, constructs a localized execution hypothesis, and mutates the global data state.

However, moving from single-turn language tasks to long-running agent chains highlights a fundamental limitation in current alignment algorithms. While fine-tuning paradigms like Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) or Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) control text distributions effectively in isolated turns, they fail to prevent error propagation across deep multi-agent networks. We define this failure mode as \textbf{semantic drift}: the compounding geometric decay of structural constraints as stochastic language variance ripples through consecutive handoff operations.

As an initial objective instruction passes through multiple agent boundaries, subtle word variances alter the latent semantics of the prompt buffer. This information decay acts as systemic background noise. Downstream execution nodes misinterpret the original boundaries, drifting into flawed tool selections or generating inaccurate SQL queries while still producing structurally valid syntax. Traditional evaluation tools, such as text-overlap metrics (BLEU, ROUGE) or expensive LLM-as-a-judge patterns, cannot catch these errors in real time. They process surface-level text strings rather than evaluating information entropy changes across high-dimensional token spaces.

Compounding this alignment issue is the challenge of real-world data governance. Running prompt compilers over modern data architectures requires exposing detailed catalog metadata, columns, and business rules to the underlying foundation models. Directly injecting these data security profiles into the context prompt scales up token consumption and creates severe attention blur, degrading model accuracy. 

To address these twin challenges of information drift and context bloat, this paper introduces a unified framework for multi-agent optimization and validation:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{The Semantic Trust Index ($\STI$):} A formal mathematical layout that measures structural information decay across agent chains by evaluating cosine divergence changes alongside real-time token Shannon entropy.
    \item \textbf{The Governance-Aware Semantic Compiler (GASC):} A programmatic compiler module that models access control lists as sparse matrices, dynamically pruning metadata prompts to drop token overhead while maintaining full data compliance.
    \item \textbf{The Dual-Route Vision Auditor (DRVA):} A multimodal vision-language framework designed to catch and suppress cross-modal visual hallucinations in business analytics dashboards.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of this manuscript is organized as follows. Section 2 reviews related work in multi-agent alignment and data catalogs. Section 3 details the mathematical derivation of our trust metrics, while Section 4 outlines our GASC compiler implementation. Section 5 presents comprehensive empirical evaluations on the Spider 2.0 and AgentBench benchmarks, followed by our ablation studies in Section 6.

